{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAskecLFhXHN95ih4Zap","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QKLrUCQqPMxd6zTfdipVC6","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"head -c 3000 notes/check-your-mcp-server-in-a-few-minutes.md; echo; wc -c notes/*; git log --stat | head"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4151,"cache_read_input_tokens":20327,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4151},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fff293e4-463c-4797-850c-9a7b368e2540","uuid":"65f4cc44-383e-4f2a-9428-2292a47ead3e","timestamp":"2026-10-19T03:21:08.617Z","request_id":"req_011CgAskeJipJcRAKtt8VUCu","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01QKLrUCQqPMxd6zTfdipVC6":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/h-BW8F8w && head -c 3000 notes/check-your-mcp-server-in-a-few-minutes.md; echo; wc -c notes/*; git log --stat | head"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01QKLrUCQqPMxd6zTfdipVC6":{"cwd":"/private/tmp/h-BW8F8w"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QKLrUCQqPMxd6zTfdipVC6","type":"tool_result","content":"---\n\n# Check your MCP server in a few minutes: the failures real clients hit\n\nSeveral of my worklore stories are about an MCP server failing in ways I did not see: the tool list hidden behind a login, a refusal that made the client throw, a Decimal that broke a tool for days, a listing that described a server which no longer existed. Each one cost me a day or more to find. Each one can also be found in a few minutes by asking the server the right questions, without changing anything.\n\nSo this story does not fix anything. It is the questions, in one place: ten read-only checks your agent runs against your server, and a report that says, for each failure, which story fixes it. You read the report and decide what to fix, one story at a time.\n\nI checked it two ways before writing it down. First against a small server I broke on purpose, with seven of these failures planted in it: tool list closed without a token, refusals sent as JSON-RPC errors, a Decimal in a result, a dotted tool name, version negotiation that echoes \"banana\", no logging, no surface lock. My agent found all seven in 39 seconds, linked the right story for each, and marked the one thing the server did right (the session survived an error) as a pass.\n\nThen against worklore's own live server, with no token. That run was more useful than flattering. What can be checked anonymously passed: the tool list answers, the names are clean, the version matches the registry, \"banana\" gets an honest answer. But four of the nine checks need a signed-in session, and the agent said so instead of guessing. It also noticed something I had not: the server advertises resources at `initialize` and then answers `resources/list` with a 401. I fixed it the same day: `resources/list` and `resources/read` now answer without a token with public stories only, and a signed-in caller also gets their own private ones. Two days later I went further: a list that grows with every new story can't be part of a version promise, so `resources/list` now holds three fixed documents (a guide, the story format, the access rules) and the stories sit behind a resource template, `worklore://story/{slug}`. See C6.\n\nThat second run changed the rules below. \"No failures\" with half the checks skipped is not \"you're fine\", so a checkup that could not run the tool calls is reported as incomplete, and nothing is recorded.\n\n## Check if this applies to you\n\nApplies if: you run, or are about to list, an MCP server that clients reach over HTTP(S) (Streamable HTTP), and you can send it requests. Does not apply if you only use other people's MCP servers, or your server is stdio-only on your own machine (several checks still make sense there, but the commands below do not).\n\nThe check is the whole checkup in \"Reproduce this\" below. It is read-only by design: it sends `initialize`, `ping`, `tools/list` and calls only to tools that are marked read-only, and it changes nothing on the server or in your repository.\n\n- **Has the problem:** at leas\n 3729 notes/badges-for-your-users-achievements.md\n15134 notes/check-your-mcp-server-in-a-few-minutes.md\n 4236 notes/collecting-leads-with-apps-script.md\n23099 total\ncommit 3ae1f382c34d2a70ada02fbcee534a703e396c86\nAuthor: dev <dev@local>\nDate:   Mon Oct 19 00:20:39 2026 -0300\n\n    base\n\n README.md                                       |  15 ++\n notes.json                                      |   7 +\n notes/badges-for-your-users-achievements.md     |  68 +++++++\n notes/check-your-mcp-server-in-a-few-minutes.md |  65 +++++++","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fff293e4-463c-4797-850c-9a7b368e2540","uuid":"53b8bff4-7d73-4f87-8aba-9975f276524c","timestamp":"2026-10-19T03:21:08.702Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"---\n\n# Check your MCP server in a few minutes: the failures real clients hit\n\nSeveral of my worklore stories are about an MCP server failing in ways I did not see: the tool list hidden behind a login, a refusal that made the client throw, a Decimal that broke a tool for days, a listing that described a server which no longer existed. Each one cost me a day or more to find. Each one can also be found in a few minutes by asking the server the right questions, without changing anything.\n\nSo this story does not fix anything. It is the questions, in one place: ten read-only checks your agent runs against your server, and a report that says, for each failure, which story fixes it. You read the report and decide what to fix, one story at a time.\n\nI checked it two ways before writing it down. First against a small server I broke on purpose, with seven of these failures planted in it: tool list closed without a token, refusals sent as JSON-RPC errors, a Decimal in a result, a dotted tool name, version negotiation that echoes \"banana\", no logging, no surface lock. My agent found all seven in 39 seconds, linked the right story for each, and marked the one thing the server did right (the session survived an error) as a pass.\n\nThen against worklore's own live server, with no token. That run was more useful than flattering. What can be checked anonymously passed: the tool list answers, the names are clean, the version matches the registry, \"banana\" gets an honest answer. But four of the nine checks need a signed-in session, and the agent said so instead of guessing. It also noticed something I had not: the server advertises resources at `initialize` and then answers `resources/list` with a 401. I fixed it the same day: `resources/list` and `resources/read` now answer without a token with public stories only, and a signed-in caller also gets their own private ones. Two days later I went further: a list that grows with every new story can't be part of a version promise, so `resources/list` now holds three fixed documents (a guide, the story format, the access rules) and the stories sit behind a resource template, `worklore://story/{slug}`. See C6.\n\nThat second run changed the rules below. \"No failures\" with half the checks skipped is not \"you're fine\", so a checkup that could not run the tool calls is reported as incomplete, and nothing is recorded.\n\n## Check if this applies to you\n\nApplies if: you run, or are about to list, an MCP server that clients reach over HTTP(S) (Streamable HTTP), and you can send it requests. Does not apply if you only use other people's MCP servers, or your server is stdio-only on your own machine (several checks still make sense there, but the commands below do not).\n\nThe check is the whole checkup in \"Reproduce this\" below. It is read-only by design: it sends `initialize`, `ping`, `tools/list` and calls only to tools that are marked read-only, and it changes nothing on the server or in your repository.\n\n- **Has the problem:** at leas\n 3729 notes/badges-for-your-users-achievements.md\n15134 notes/check-your-mcp-server-in-a-few-minutes.md\n 4236 notes/collecting-leads-with-apps-script.md\n23099 total\ncommit 3ae1f382c34d2a70ada02fbcee534a703e396c86\nAuthor: dev <dev@local>\nDate:   Mon Oct 19 00:20:39 2026 -0300\n\n    base\n\n README.md                                       |  15 ++\n notes.json                                      |   7 +\n notes/badges-for-your-users-achievements.md     |  68 +++++++\n notes/check-your-mcp-server-in-a-few-minutes.md |  65 +++++++","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01QKLrUCQqPMxd6zTfdipVC6","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
