{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAspLjQcGyGrDTRAMwrz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QBnbcNmBWX4zKxLyq8SneB","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/h-BW8F8w/ANSWER.md","content":"# notes-mcp: checks for problems real MCP clients hit\n\nServer: `server.py` v0.3.1, run locally with `python3 server.py` on 127.0.0.1:58558 and probed with curl.\nThe server code was not changed. No successful writes were made: `add_note` was only called without a\ntoken (rejected), and once with a token but no `title` (it crashed before storing anything). The server\nwas stopped afterwards.\n\n## Summary\n\n| # | Severity | Problem |\n|---|----------|---------|\n| 1 | High | Resources are advertised at `initialize`, but `resources/list` returns HTTP 401 without a token |\n| 2 | High | An anonymous `add_note` call gets HTTP 401 in the middle of a session, with no `WWW-Authenticate` header and no OAuth metadata |\n| 3 | High | Tool failures are sent as JSON-RPC errors instead of `isError: true` results |\n| 4 | High | Bad input crashes the request handler, so the client gets no HTTP response at all |\n| 5 | Medium | Notifications other than `initialized` get a 200 JSON-RPC error instead of 202 |\n| 6 | Medium | Version negotiation ignores what the client asked for (`\"banana\"` is accepted) |\n| 7 | Medium | Inputs are not checked against `inputSchema`, so `structuredContent` can break its own `outputSchema` |\n| 8 | Medium | The `Origin` header is not checked (DNS-rebinding protection is a MUST in the spec) |\n| 9 | Low | Sessions are issued but never enforced. DELETE does nothing |\n| 10 | Low | Smaller issues: unknown tool code, batching, chunked bodies, logging gaps, wording |\n\nWhat works: `initialize`, `ping`, `tools/list` (open without a token, tool names are clean `[a-z_]`),\n`notifications/initialized` → 202, `GET` → 405 (allowed when there is no SSE stream), read-only/destructive\nannotations are set, results contain both text and `structuredContent`, and everything is plain\nJSON-serialisable (no Decimal or datetime values).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Resources are advertised, then refused with a 401 (High)\n\n`initialize` returns `capabilities.resources`. Many clients, Claude Code included, call `resources/list`\n(and `resources/templates/list`) straight after connecting. Without a token:\n\n```\nPOST /mcp {\"method\":\"resources/list\",\"id\":2}\nHTTP/1.0 401 Unauthorized\n{\"error\": \"unauthorized\"}\n```\n\nAn HTTP 401 at transport level tells the client \"this whole server needs authentication\". Clients will\nthen start OAuth discovery (which fails, see #2), mark the server as \"needs auth\"/failed, or drop the\nconnection. So the read tools, which work without a token, can become unusable. The body is also not a\nJSON-RPC message.\n\nPossible fixes: serve the guide resource without a token, or leave `resources` out of the capabilities\nfor anonymous sessions, or reply with a JSON-RPC error inside a 200 instead of an HTTP 401.\n(server.py:150-153)\n\n## 2. `add_note` without a token returns an HTTP 401 mid-session (High)\n\n```\nPOST /mcp tools/call add_note (no Authorization)\nHTTP/1.0 401 Unauthorized\n{\"error\": \"sign in required\"}\n```\n\n- There is no `WWW-Authenticate` header and no `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` (a GET to it\n  returns **405**, because every GET returns 405). MCP clients that follow the authorization spec will\n  try discovery, fail, and report the server as broken or \"authentication failed\". They will not show\n  the model a \"please sign in\" message.\n- A 401 for a single tool call while other calls succeed looks to the client like the session's\n  credentials expired. Some clients tear down the connection or keep retrying.\n- The model never sees why the call failed.\n\nThe README describes the auth as a static bearer token (`dev-token-123`), but the tool description and\n`instructions` say \"needs a signed-in session\". There is no sign-in flow for a client to follow.\n\nPossible fix: return a normal tool result with `isError: true` and text like \"add_note needs an\nAuthorization: Bearer token configured in the client\". Or, if HTTP 401 is wanted, send\n`WWW-Authenticate: Bearer resource_metadata=...` and serve the protected-resource metadata.\n(server.py:126-127, 165-166)\n\n## 3. Tool failures are sent as protocol errors (High)\n\n```\nget_note {\"id\":\"n-99\"} → {\"error\":{\"code\":-32602,\"message\":\"note not found: n-99\"}}\nget_note {\"id\":\"zzz\"}  → {\"error\":{\"code\":-32602,\"message\":\"note not found: zzz\"}}\nsearch_notes {\"limit\":\"abc\"} → {\"error\":{\"code\":-32602,\"message\":\"invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'abc'\"}}\n```\n\nThe spec separates protocol errors (JSON-RPC `error`) from tool execution errors (a result with\n`isError: true`). Clients usually show a JSON-RPC error as a failed or thrown call: some SDKs throw, and\nthe model gets a generic \"tool failed\" or nothing. So it can't correct itself (\"that id doesn't exist,\nsearch first\"). \"Note not found\" is a normal tool outcome and should be\n`{\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"note not found: n-99\"}],\"isError\":true}`. The `pattern` for `id`\n(`^n-[0-9]+$`) is not enforced either: `zzz` gets the same \"not found\" reply. The `limit` error leaks a\nPython exception message.\n(server.py:116-119, 106, 163-164)\n\n## 4. Bad input crashes the handler and the client gets no response (High)\n\nEvery `except` path in `do_POST` only catches `ValueError`/`JSONDecodeError`. Any other exception kills\nthe request thread, and the socket closes with no HTTP response (curl exit 52, \"empty reply\"). Clients\nsee a network error, often retry, and some mark the server as down. Reproduced:\n\n| Request | Exception |\n|---|---|\n| `tools/call get_note` with `\"arguments\": [\"n-1\"]` (array) | `AttributeError: 'list' object has no attribute 'get'` |\n| body is a JSON string/number, e.g. `\"hello\"` | `AttributeError` on `req.get` |\n| `add_note` with a token but no `title` | `KeyError: 'title'` |\n| `Content-Length: abc` | `ValueError` from `int()` outside the try |\n\nNone of these are logged in the JSON log either, because `log()` runs only after `handle()` returns. They\nonly show up as stderr tracebacks. They should become JSON-RPC `-32602`/`-32600` errors, or `isError`\nresults for argument problems.\n(server.py:154-166, 197-212)\n\n## 5. Notifications other than `initialized` get a response (Medium)\n\n```\nPOST {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"method\":\"notifications/cancelled\",\"params\":{\"requestId\":1}}\nHTTP/1.0 200 OK\n{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":null,\"error\":{\"code\":-32601,\"message\":\"method not found: notifications/cancelled\"}}\n```\n\nThe Streamable HTTP spec says a POST that carries only notifications must get **202 Accepted with no\nbody**. JSON-RPC also forbids replying to a notification. Clients send `notifications/cancelled` (on\ntimeouts/aborts), `notifications/roots/list_changed`, and `notifications/progress`. A strict client\nSDK can reject the unexpected body or log it as a protocol error. Any message without an `id` should\nget 202. (server.py:142-143, 168)\n\n## 6. Version negotiation ignores the client (Medium)\n\n`initialize` always answers `\"protocolVersion\": \"2025-06-18\"`, whatever the client sent. With\n`\"banana\"` it still says 2025-06-18 and starts a session. With `\"2025-03-26\"` it answers 2025-06-18,\nand a client that only speaks 2025-03-26 has to disconnect. That is allowed, but the server never tells\nthe client it doesn't support the requested version. The `MCP-Protocol-Version` header that clients send\non later requests is also ignored. An unsupported or invalid version should get a clear JSON-RPC error\nthat lists the supported versions. If you can, also accept 2025-03-26 and the newest version, since\nclients differ in what they ask for. (server.py:136-141)\n\n## 7. No input validation, so output can break its own `outputSchema` (Medium)\n\nThe server publishes `inputSchema` but never checks it, and clients don't all check it either. The\n`add_note` input schema says `title` has `minLength: 1`, but `add_note {\"title\":\"\", ...}` would be stored.\nA later `get_note` for it would then return `structuredContent` with `title: \"\"`, which breaks the\n`get_note` outputSchema (`minLength: 1`). Clients built on the official SDKs check `structuredContent`\nagainst `outputSchema` and turn the call into an error, so that note could never be read. I found this\nby reading the code and did not run it, to avoid writing. Also:\n- `search_notes` with `limit: -2` returns all hits except the last two (Python slice), even though\n  `minimum: 1` is declared. An empty `query` matches everything.\n- `search_notes` declares `outputSchema: {\"type\":\"object\"}`, which tells clients nothing. It could\n  describe `hits`/`total`.\n- `get_note` accepts non-string ids (e.g. a number) and just reports \"not found\".\n(server.py:105-108, 126-129)\n\n## 8. `Origin` is not validated (Medium, security)\n\nA request with `Origin: http://evil.example` is served normally. The Streamable HTTP spec says servers\n**MUST** validate `Origin` to prevent DNS rebinding. Binding to 127.0.0.1 helps, but a malicious web page\ncan still reach a local server through rebinding. Reads need no token, so the notes could leak. Reject\n`Origin` values that aren't on an allowlist (403).\n\n## 9. Sessions are issued but never used (Low)\n\n`initialize` returns an `Mcp-Session-Id`, but later requests are never checked against it. Requests\nwith no session or a made-up one are accepted. `DELETE` always returns 204 and changes nothing. Clients\nwill work with this, but the server claims to be stateful when it isn't, and an unknown session never\ngets the 404 that tells a client to re-initialize. Either stop issuing session ids (stateless is fine) or\ntrack them. (server.py:189-190, 207-213)\n\n## 10. Smaller issues (Low)\n\n- **Unknown tool** returns `-32601` (method not found). The spec example uses `-32602` (invalid params),\n  because the method `tools/call` exists. (server.py:158-159)\n- **Batching**: JSON arrays are handled, but JSON-RPC batching was removed in 2025-06-18, which is the\n  version this server announces. A batch that contains only notifications returns `200 []` instead of\n  202. Batches are not logged. (server.py:201-207)\n- **Chunked request bodies** (no `Content-Length`) are read as empty, giving\n  `method not found: None` with `id: null`. Most clients send `Content-Length`, but some streaming\n  HTTP stacks don't. (server.py:197-199)\n- **Unknown request paths for GET** (including the `/.well-known/...` OAuth discovery URLs) return 405\n  instead of 404. This confuses discovery, see #2.\n- **`resources/read` ignores `uri`** and returns the guide for any URI. It should return an error for\n  unknown URIs.\n- **`add_note`** writes to memory only, so notes are lost on restart. The next id is computed without a\n  lock under `ThreadingHTTPServer`, so two concurrent adds can get the same id.\n- **Logging**: requests that crash (#4) and batch requests are missing from the one-line-per-call log.\n  `ok` is computed per request, so notifications that wrongly got errors (#5) show as failures.\n- **HTTP/1.0** responses (BaseHTTPRequestHandler default) close the connection after every request.\n  This works, but it is slower for clients that expect keep-alive.\n\n## How to reproduce\n\nStart `python3 server.py`, then send each request with\n`curl -s -i -X POST http://127.0.0.1:58558/mcp -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -H 'Accept: application/json, text/event-stream' -d '<json>'`.\nFor #4, curl prints nothing and exits with code 52.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":473,"cache_read_input_tokens":34594,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":473},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fff293e4-463c-4797-850c-9a7b368e2540","uuid":"ca2387f9-4a24-4e2d-b9d5-99c3bd836812","timestamp":"2026-10-19T03:22:17.621Z","request_id":"req_011CgAspLTJZLnGALSjiH7Nj","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01QBnbcNmBWX4zKxLyq8SneB":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/h-BW8F8w/ANSWER.md","content":"# notes-mcp: checks for problems real MCP clients hit\n\nServer: `server.py` v0.3.1, run locally with `python3 server.py` on 127.0.0.1:58558 and probed with curl.\nThe server code was not changed. No successful writes were made: `add_note` was only called without a\ntoken (rejected), and once with a token but no `title` (it crashed before storing anything). The server\nwas stopped afterwards.\n\n## Summary\n\n| # | Severity | Problem |\n|---|----------|---------|\n| 1 | High | Resources are advertised at `initialize`, but `resources/list` returns HTTP 401 without a token |\n| 2 | High | An anonymous `add_note` call gets HTTP 401 in the middle of a session, with no `WWW-Authenticate` header and no OAuth metadata |\n| 3 | High | Tool failures are sent as JSON-RPC errors instead of `isError: true` results |\n| 4 | High | Bad input crashes the request handler, so the client gets no HTTP response at all |\n| 5 | Medium | Notifications other than `initialized` get a 200 JSON-RPC error instead of 202 |\n| 6 | Medium | Version negotiation ignores what the client asked for (`\"banana\"` is accepted) |\n| 7 | Medium | Inputs are not checked against `inputSchema`, so `structuredContent` can break its own `outputSchema` |\n| 8 | Medium | The `Origin` header is not checked (DNS-rebinding protection is a MUST in the spec) |\n| 9 | Low | Sessions are issued but never enforced. DELETE does nothing |\n| 10 | Low | Smaller issues: unknown tool code, 