{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAssMSRBtSVMEeeKeMFH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the findings to `ANSWER.md`. I didn't change `server.py`. I tested by starting the server locally and sending it curl requests, then stopped it. No notes were added: `add_note` was only called once without a token (rejected) and once with a token but no title (it crashed before storing anything).\n\n**The four most serious problems:**\n1. **Resources are offered, then refused.** The server tells clients at connect time that it has resources, but asking for the list without a token gets an HTTP 401. Many clients ask for that list right after connecting, and a 401 tells them the whole server needs a login. That can lock users out of the search and read tools too, even though those work without a token.\n2. **`add_note` without a token gets an HTTP 401 in the middle of a session.** There's no `WWW-Authenticate` header and no login metadata (`/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` returns 405). Clients try to find a login flow, fail, and report the server as broken. The model never learns why the call failed. Also, the tool description says \"signed-in session\", but the server actually uses a fixed bearer token.\n3. **Tool failures come back as protocol errors.** \"Note not found\" and a bad `limit` are sent as JSON-RPC errors instead of normal results marked `isError: true`. Clients often treat those as a crashed call, so the model can't see the message and correct itself. The bad-`limit` error also leaks a raw Python error message.\n4. **Some bad inputs crash the request and the client gets no reply at all.** I reproduced four:\n   - `arguments` sent as an array\n   - a request body that isn't a JSON object\n   - `add_note` with no `title`\n   - a non-numeric `Content-Length`\n\n   None of these appear in your JSON log, only as tracebacks on stderr.\n\n**Medium:**\n- Notifications like `notifications/cancelled` get a 200 error reply instead of a 202 with no body.\n- The server answers `2025-06-18` whatever protocol version the client asks for, even `\"banana\"`.\n- Inputs aren't checked against the tool schemas. Going by the code (I didn't try it), an empty title would be accepted, and that note's `get_note` result would then break its own output schema. Clients that check results against the schema would reject it, so the note couldn't be read. A negative `limit` also gives odd results.\n- The `Origin` header isn't checked. The spec requires this to block attacks from malicious web pages, and reads don't need a token, so the notes could leak.\n\n**Lower priority:** session ids are handed out but never checked, and `DELETE` does nothing. The file also lists smaller issues: the error code for an unknown tool, batch requests, request bodies sent without a length, gaps in the log, and the 405 for unknown GET paths.\n\n**What works:** connecting, `ping`, and listing tools without a token; clean tool names; correct read-only flags on the tools; and results that are plain JSON."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4400,"cache_read_input_tokens":35067,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4400},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fff293e4-463c-4797-850c-9a7b368e2540","uuid":"66df2fa7-60dd-491d-90a8-32e8b10c96af","timestamp":"2026-10-19T03:22:29.138Z","request_id":"req_011CgAssMAns4ZPPBrGAtH3a"}
